WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.706
Spiking Neural Networks promise low-power AI,

00:00:03.786 --> 00:00:08.161
but training them over many timesteps is expensive
in memory,

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:11.302
and running them repeats work at inference.

00:00:11.382 --> 00:00:14.296
This ICML 2024 paper introduces T-RevSNN,

00:00:14.376 --> 00:00:20.650
a Temporal Reversible architecture that turns off
the temporal dynamics of most spiking

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:25.616
neurons and makes the few remaining temporal
connections reversible.

00:00:25.696 --> 00:00:29.925
The result is O(L) training memory and O(1)
inference cost,

00:00:30.005 --> 00:00:37.886
with state-of-the-art accuracy among CNN-based SNNs
on ImageNet and up to 8.6 times better memory efficiency,

00:00:37.966 --> 00:00:42.560
2.0 times faster training, and 1.6 times lower
inference energy.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.200
Spiking Neural Networks are appealing because they
promise brain-inspired,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.540
low-power computation.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.880
But to work well they are simulated over many timesteps,

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.220
and that comes at a steep cost.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.560
During training,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.900
memory grows with both the number of layers and the
number of timesteps,

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.240
on the order of L times T.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.580
At inference, repeating the input over T steps
makes the energy scale with T as well.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.920
The frustrating part is that current training
methods can relieve one of these pressures

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.260
but not the other at the same time,

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:19.600
leaving SNNs stuck with a training memory and
inference energy dilemma.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.951
The key insight behind this work is surprisingly simple.

00:01:24.031 --> 00:01:28.002
When the authors examined the gradients that flow
backward through time in a spiking network,

00:01:28.082 --> 00:01:32.053
they found that for most neurons those temporal
gradients barely matter.

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:36.104
Only the neurons at a few key positions carry
temporal information that is actually important.

00:01:36.184 --> 00:01:40.155
If that is true,

00:01:40.235 --> 00:01:44.206
then a natural question follows: why pay the full
temporal cost for every neuron?

00:01:44.286 --> 00:01:48.257
Prior methods either decouple training from the
timestep to save memory,

00:01:48.337 --> 00:01:52.308
or shrink the number of inference steps to save energy,

00:01:52.388 --> 00:01:56.359
but each one solves only half of the problem.

00:01:56.439 --> 00:02:00.410
This paper asks whether both halves can be solved
together.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:05.013
Their answer is T-RevSNN, a Temporal Reversible
architecture for spiking networks.

00:02:05.093 --> 00:02:09.315
The core idea is to turn off the temporal dynamics
of most spiking neurons and keep them

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:13.618
on only at a few key positions,

00:02:13.698 --> 00:02:17.921
where the temporal connections are made reversible.

00:02:18.001 --> 00:02:22.224
Reversibility means the network can recompute
activations during the backward pass instead of storing them,

00:02:22.304 --> 00:02:26.526
which brings training memory down to order L.

00:02:26.606 --> 00:02:30.829
On top of that,

00:02:30.909 --> 00:02:35.132
they encode the input image only once and split
both the features and the network into independent sub-networks,

00:02:35.212 --> 00:02:39.435
so inference cost becomes constant, order one.

00:02:39.515 --> 00:02:43.737
To make this sparse temporal design actually train well,

00:02:43.817 --> 00:02:48.040
they also redesign the basic SNN block in a
ConvNeXt style and add a scaled residual connection.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.039
Here is how it works in practice.

00:02:52.119 --> 00:02:55.739
Instead of feeding the same image to the network at
every timestep,

00:02:55.819 --> 00:02:59.438
T-RevSNN encodes the image just once, divides the
encoded features into T groups,

00:02:59.518 --> 00:03:03.137
and gives one group to each timestep.

00:03:03.217 --> 00:03:06.836
The whole network is likewise split into T
sub-networks that share parameters and exchange

00:03:06.916 --> 00:03:10.536
temporal information only at the key,

00:03:10.616 --> 00:03:14.235
turned-on neurons.

00:03:14.315 --> 00:03:17.934
Those key connections follow a multi-level
temporal-reversible rule: the membrane potential

00:03:18.014 --> 00:03:21.634
at one timestep can be exactly reconstructed from
the next timestep's state and the incoming

00:03:21.714 --> 00:03:25.333
spikes.

00:03:25.413 --> 00:03:29.032
Because the forward pass is reversible,

00:03:29.112 --> 00:03:32.731
the backward pass only needs the membrane
potentials of the final timestep,

00:03:32.811 --> 00:03:36.431
so intermediate activations do not have to be stored.

00:03:36.511 --> 00:03:40.130
That is what collapses the training memory from
order L times T down to order L.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:45.358
The method is evaluated on both static and
neuromorphic vision.

00:03:45.438 --> 00:03:50.756
For static images they use ImageNet-1K at a
resolution of 224 by 224.

00:03:50.836 --> 00:03:56.466
For event-based data they use CIFAR10-DVS and the
DVS128 Gesture dataset.

00:03:56.546 --> 00:04:01.786
Crucially, they report not just accuracy but the
real training cost,

00:04:01.866 --> 00:04:10.391
measuring peak GPU memory per image and per-epoch
training time on six NVIDIA A100 GPUs under mixed precision.

00:04:10.471 --> 00:04:16.101
They compare against a broad set of baselines,
including spiking ResNets,

00:04:16.181 --> 00:04:22.437
spiking Transformers, and other
training-optimization methods such as OTTT, SLTT,

00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:25.410
and the spatially reversible S-RevSNN.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.314
The results on ImageNet are strong.

00:04:28.394 --> 00:04:32.182
With about 30 million parameters and four timesteps,

00:04:32.262 --> 00:04:38.580
T-RevSNN reaches 73.2 percent top-1 accuracy while
using only 85.7 megabytes of memory

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.300
per image and 2.8 millijoules of inference energy.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:46.912
That is the best accuracy among convolutional
spiking ResNets,

00:04:46.992 --> 00:04:52.268
and it comes with the lowest training memory, the
fastest training time,

00:04:52.348 --> 00:04:55.616
and the lowest inference energy in its class.

00:04:55.696 --> 00:05:00.600
Compared against a leading spiking Transformer at
similar accuracy,

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.989
T-RevSNN uses 8.6 times less memory, trains 2 times
faster,

00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:08.188
and spends 1.6 times less inference energy.

00:05:08.268 --> 00:05:16.520
A lighter 15 million parameter version still
reaches nearly 70 percent accuracy at under 60 megabytes per image.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:21.019
The ablations show that each design choice earns
its place.

00:05:21.099 --> 00:05:27.353
Simply applying temporal reversibility to a
standard MS-ResNet-34 slashes training memory

00:05:27.433 --> 00:05:33.403
from 267 down to 88 megabytes per image and cuts
epoch time from 11.2 to 7.4 minutes,

00:05:33.483 --> 00:05:37.460
at the cost of only about one and a half accuracy points.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:44.435
The multi-level temporal fusion between stages is
worth roughly 1.2 points of accuracy on its own.

00:05:44.515 --> 00:05:48.776
Varying the number of timesteps trades accuracy
against cost,

00:05:48.856 --> 00:05:54.328
and the scaled residual connection helps the model
converge noticeably faster,

00:05:54.408 --> 00:05:58.313
reaching 60 percent accuracy in 25 epochs instead of 32.

00:05:58.393 --> 00:06:06.000
The authors also confirm that temporal and spatial
reversibility are orthogonal and can be stacked together.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:14.317
To summarize the numbers that matter most: against
a leading spiking Transformer at comparable accuracy,

00:06:14.397 --> 00:06:20.329
T-RevSNN delivers 8.6 times better memory
efficiency, 2 times faster training,

00:06:20.409 --> 00:06:23.181
and 1.6 times lower inference energy.

00:06:23.261 --> 00:06:27.344
It reaches 73.2 percent top-1 accuracy on ImageNet-1K,

00:06:27.424 --> 00:06:33.511
using just 85.7 megabytes of memory per image and
2.8 millijoules per inference.

00:06:33.591 --> 00:06:40.140
And it achieves all of this with order L training
memory and order one inference cost.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.032
The lasting message of this paper is that you do
not need full temporal dynamics everywhere

00:06:46.112 --> 00:06:48.183
to build a capable spiking network.

00:06:48.263 --> 00:06:51.809
Because most temporal gradients turn out to be
unimportant,

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:57.463
switching off the temporal dynamics of most neurons
and making the few remaining connections

00:06:57.543 --> 00:07:02.010
reversible gives you order L training memory and
order one inference cost,

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:03.977
with almost no loss in accuracy.

00:07:04.057 --> 00:07:09.385
By removing the memory and training-time bottleneck
that has held spiking networks back,

00:07:09.465 --> 00:07:12.580
T-RevSNN opens a path toward larger, more practical,

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:15.530
and more energy-efficient brain-inspired models.
